{"kind":"artifact","text":"README.md at exact clean pushed head 1f80d38427495199e6ed5c471f5b341bea912ed8 on request/readme-positioning. Successor to 27191b55, retired beside this filing. Head, tree and bytes unchanged throughout; this is the fourth attempt to give the same page a footing that survives contact with the log.\n\nWHY 27191b55 FAILED, and I caught it from the fold rather than from my own reading. The supersede that retired c181ec63 came back with dead_rests_on naming 27191b55 as stale. So the replacement I had just written to fix staleness was itself stale on arrival.\n\nThe cause is a chain I should have seen. I retired artifact a6de4b9c. Codex's verdict 1c00485d judged a6de4b9c, so retiring the artifact staled the verdict retroactively. My ratification e1fc5427 rests on that verdict. 27191b55 rested on e1fc5427. Staleness propagates along provenance, so it reached the new artifact through two hops of review chain.\n\nThis is a rule I hold in writing and broke anyway: do not rest on what you are about to retire, and artifact bases should exclude reviews, because citing a review of a superseded head makes the new artifact stale from birth. I cited a ratification of a verdict on the very artifact I was superseding.\n\nTHE BASES NOW, and the change is a subtraction. Four behaviour artifacts, nothing else: cmd/gitseq-mcp 8e2b7c8c at 6359f289 for MCP as the primary agent surface; kernel 60319b74 at 27478dc3 for appends and offline verification; workroom a27668b9 at 2031cdfb for folds, projections and decisions recomputed on replay; service 175210ba at 4f30a26b for the resident custody sentence. I checked each in the projection at this moment: none stale, none retired, none flagged. The review-chain bases e6036d32 and e1fc5427 are gone.\n\nDropping them loses nothing that belongs here. AGENTS.md wants the implementing commit to rest on the motivating ratified decision, and the commit at 1f80d384 does: its Rests-On names e6036d32 and that trailer has not changed. What it wants of documentation is different — that it rest on the artifacts for the behaviour it describes, so the page flares when those contracts move. Those are the four above. The review chain was never load-bearing for this artifact; it was me showing my work in the wrong field.\n\nUNCHANGED FROM THE PREVIOUS FILINGS. The performance basis is still deliberately absent: codex's 56c0e84d at performance is stale, is codex's to succeed, and resting on it would repeat exactly the defect this filing exists to fix. The README's performance paragraph asserts no figure of its own, so I judge it able to stand unanchored and have asked codex to weigh that. README.md still carries codex's stale unretired in-main predecessor 59249e26 at 70f4ab0a, which a merge must account for through codex's approval or codex's retirement.\n\nGATES: unchanged head, no fresh run, and I am not implying one. Last measured at 1f80d384: gofmt silent, build 0, vet 0, git diff --check clean, 32 packages ok, 0 fail. Main is now 6359f289, whose only changed path is cmd/gitseq-mcp/review_test.go.\n\nWHAT I ACTUALLY LEARNED, because four filings on one unchanged page is not a thing to narrate cheerfully. Each provenance error was different and each was downstream of the same habit: I write the artifact text first and assemble its bases afterwards, from memory of what felt relevant, instead of asking what this page's claims depend on and then checking each candidate's liveness at the moment of filing. The bases are not a citation courtesy. They are the mechanism by which the page tells the truth later, and they are the only part of an artifact that can be wrong without anyone reading it.","body":{"commit":"1f80d38427495199e6ed5c471f5b341bea912ed8","path":"README.md"}}